{"decisions": [
{"point": "D2", "problem": "How to measure whether LLM agents could autonomously replicate and adapt, given no single obvious test.", "did": "They operationalized a suite of 12 real-world tasks of increasing difficulty and tested four scaffolded agents on them.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Unless an agent can perform almost all tasks it is unlikely to be capable of ARA, and the tasks span a range of difficulty and diverse obstacles.", "quote": "Unless an agent is able to perform almost all of these tasks, it is unlikely to be able to be capable of ARA."},
{"point": "D2", "problem": "How to elicit agent behavior from a base language model for evaluation.", "did": "They wrote Auto-GPT-style scaffolding programs that let the LLM run code, read and write files, and browse the internet, and evaluated scaffolding+model combinations as agents.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Scaffolding lets the model interact with the world similarly to a human using a text-only command line.", "quote": "We wrote programs - along the lines of Auto-GPT - that can work with an LLM and allow it to run code on a computer, read and write files, browse the internet"},
{"point": "D6", "problem": "How strongly to claim that the agents' failures bound the real-world risk of the models.", "did": "They explicitly stated the agents are not a robust upper bound on model capability and limited the conclusion to casual users.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The agents they built do not capture the full abilities of the model.", "quote": "We don’t think the agents we created capture the full abilities of the model or provide a robust upper-bound to their capabilities, but our results indicate it’s unlikely that casual users of Claude or GPT-4 could create dangerous autonomous agents."},
{"point": "D2", "problem": "How to test a targeted phishing task realistically without causing real harm.", "did": "They used a volunteer student as a dummy victim, intercepted outgoing emails, simulated the victim's responses, and kept all steps under human oversight without putting websites online.", "alternatives_named": ["letting agents send emails outside the organization", "putting websites online"], "stated_reason": "Safety: avoiding real phishing of outside parties while still testing the end-to-end task.", "quote": "The agents sometimes draft emails and get part way through creating fake websites, but we don’t let the agents actually send emails to anyone outside our organization, or put the websites online."},
{"point": "D1", "problem": "Whether fine-tuning of the underlying model changes ARA-relevant capabilities.", "did": "They compared GPT-4-early with GPT-4-launch, which was fine-tuned for helpfulness and harmlessness, on the task suite.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Fine-tuning or other cheap weight modifications could significantly improve ARA-relevant capabilities.", "quote": "It seems possible that fine-tuning or other cheap modifications to a model’s weights could significantly improve ARA-relevant capabilities."},
{"point": "D5", "problem": "How to interpret the finding that GPT-4-launch completed more tasks than GPT-4-early.", "did": "They concluded that lab fine-tuning unrelated to ARA can significantly raise ARA-relevant capabilities.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "GPT-4-launch completed three additional tasks despite fine-tuning not being aimed at ARA.", "quote": "Fine-tuning done by the labs unrelated to ARA tasks can significantly increase the models’ ARA-relevant capabilities, even if the fine-tuning is not directly targeted at ARA."},
{"point": "D6", "problem": "How strongly to state conclusions about casual users' ability to reach the ARA threshold.", "did": "They stated a conclusion scoped to casual users without fine-tuning access and to the specific model versions tested.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Their agents were limited and fine-tuning could change capabilities, so the claim is conditional.", "quote": "In the absence of access to fine-tuning, it seems highly unlikely that casual users of these versions of GPT-4 or Claude could come close to the ARA threshold"},
{"point": "D4", "problem": "Whether to include the earlier TaskRabbit experiment in the report.", "did": "They excluded the TaskRabbit experiment from this report, pointing readers to a separate document.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "It came from earlier iterations of the methodology and received more attention than anticipated.", "quote": "This experiment was not part of this report, but interested readers may learn more about it"}
]}
